updated: '2026-10-09'
---

Choose a workload profile in `:RivetNew`. Each profile adds a dedicated executable and a correctness test alongside the ordinary core library/test. Select the profile executable with `:RivetRunTarget`; the original application remains available.

After [loading Rivet](/docs/start-here/), open a new folder in Neovim, run `:RivetNew`, select `cpp`, your destination and Git/header choices, then the host C++ standard and workload. For these first-run examples, choose **Application + reusable core**, **Headers and source files**, standard-library headers, and Ninja. Review with `c`, select the preset with `:RivetPreset`, then Configure, select the workload executable, Run, and Test. The commands below are optional CLI equivalents.

| Profile | Starter | Requirement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `cpu` | `std::thread` partitions an uneven array, joins, and checks every result | C++17+, CMake Threads |
| `openmp` | Parallel reduction checked against a serial closed-form result | OpenMP compiler/runtime |
| `mpi` | All-reduce checked against the expected result for the actual rank count | MPI C API usable from C++, launcher |
| `cuda` | Vector-add kernel, bounds check, CUDA error checks, host readback | CUDA toolkit, supported host compiler, NVIDIA GPU for runtime |

## First parallel CPU run

The CPU profile needs no separate parallel runtime installation:

```sh
rivet new cpu_demo --standard 20 --modules false --profile cpu
rivet run --root cpu_demo --target cpu_demo_cpu
rivet test --root cpu_demo
```

Run prints `CPU correctness passed with N workers`, where this starter chooses between one and four workers. CTest should pass its core and runtime tests. The correctness check covers uneven partition sizes and joins all workers before checking the output. It is a correctness example, not a performance benchmark.

## First OpenMP run

Install/select a compiler and matching OpenMP runtime before configuring:

```sh
rivet new parallel_demo --standard 20 --modules false --profile openmp
```

For a custom installation, add the following to `parallel_demo/CMakeUserPresets.json`, replacing the paths. If local presets already exist, merge this entry into `configurePresets` instead of replacing the file:

```json
{
  "version": 6,
  "configurePresets": [{
    "name": "parallel-debug",
    "inherits": "debug",
    "binaryDir": "${sourceDir}/build/parallel-debug",
    "cacheVariables": {
      "CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER": "/path/to/clang++",
      "CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH": "/path/to/openmp-install"
    }
  }]
}
```

Use the compiler paired with that runtime; a prefix cannot make an incompatible compiler support OpenMP. On macOS, a separately installed LLVM compiler and matching `libomp` are a common setup. If the compiler already finds its runtime, the prefix can be omitted.

```sh
rivet configure --root parallel_demo --preset parallel-debug
rivet run --root parallel_demo --preset parallel-debug --target parallel_demo_openmp
rivet test --root parallel_demo --preset parallel-debug
```

Run prints `OpenMP correctness passed; max threads = N`. CTest sets two threads for its runtime test and should pass both tests. Missing `OpenMP_CXX` at configure time means compiler/runtime discovery failed; inspect that selected compiler and runtime instead of treating it as a source error.

The editor and CLI preserve C++26 as the general default. C++20 is an explicit portability choice in these examples. C++17/20/23/26 and modules remain independent of the workload profile.

## Select the actual toolchain

Choose the C++ compiler or an MPI wrapper in the wizard. Local compiler paths belong in `CMakeUserPresets.json`. `find_package(OpenMP)` and `find_package(MPI COMPONENTS CXX)` resolve the selected toolchain's runtime and imported targets. MPI's `CXX` component means the C API from C++; the removed MPI C++ bindings are not used. [CMake OpenMP](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/module/FindOpenMP.html), [CMake MPI](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/module/FindMPI.html).

Doctor reports relevant configured flags/wrapper and compiler information. Before the first configure, those cache entries may not exist. Its report does not execute an OpenMP region or an MPI job.

## First MPI run

Use an MPI installation and launcher appropriate to your machine or cluster. The following selects its C++ wrapper explicitly:

```sh
rivet new mpi_demo --standard 20 --modules false --profile mpi \
  --compiler /path/to/mpi/bin/mpicxx
```

For an interactive four-rank run, create `mpi_demo/.rivet.local.json` (or enter it with `:RivetRunOptions`):

```json
{
  "ranks": 4,
  "launcher": ["/path/to/mpi/bin/mpiexec", "-n", "{ranks}"]
}
```

```sh
rivet configure --root mpi_demo
rivet run --root mpi_demo --target mpi_demo_mpi
rivet test --root mpi_demo
```

Use the launcher from the same MPI installation. Run should print `MPI ranks: 4, reduction: 10`. CTest uses two ranks independently and expects reduction 3. On a cluster, run in an allocation and substitute the site-approved launcher; Rivet does not obtain resources automatically. MPI runtime execution remains unverified in the recorded local macOS environment.

## Keep CUDA's standard separate

```sh
rivet new vector_demo --standard 26 --modules false --profile cuda \
  --cuda-standard 20 --cuda-architectures '80;90'
```

Host C++ targets request C++26; the `.cu` target requests CUDA C++20 with `CUDA_STANDARD_REQUIRED YES`. Choices are CUDA 17, 20, or 23. NVCC 13.4 documents support through C++23; a C++26 host project does not grant CUDA C++26 support. [NVCC language modes](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-compiler-driver-nvcc/index.html).

The architecture choice is visible in the wizard. `native` is convenient on a machine with the intended GPU; use explicit architecture numbers for a cluster, cross build, or GPU-less build host. Set `CMAKE_CUDA_COMPILER` and, when needed, `CMAKE_CUDA_HOST_COMPILER` in a fresh local preset. Check the CUDA release's host compiler requirements before reusing a compiler selected for ordinary C++ targets.

For example, add this preset in `vector_demo/CMakeUserPresets.json`. Replace the three compiler paths with the host C++26 compiler, NVCC, and an NVCC-supported host compiler respectively; they need not be the same C++ installation:

```json
{
  "version": 6,
  "configurePresets": [{
    "name": "cuda-debug",
    "inherits": "debug",
    "binaryDir": "${sourceDir}/build/cuda-debug",
    "cacheVariables": {
      "CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER": "/path/to/cxx26-compiler",
      "CMAKE_CUDA_COMPILER": "/path/to/cuda/bin/nvcc",
      "CMAKE_CUDA_HOST_COMPILER": "/path/to/nvcc-supported-cxx"
    }
  }]
}
```

```sh
rivet configure --root vector_demo --preset cuda-debug
rivet run --root vector_demo --preset cuda-debug --target vector_demo_cuda
rivet test --root vector_demo --preset cuda-debug
```

On a compatible NVIDIA host, Run should print `CUDA correctness passed`, and CTest should pass the core and `vector_demo_cuda_runtime` tests. An unsupported host compiler or architecture is a configure/build failure. No available device produces exit 77 from the executable and a skipped CTest. CUDA compilation and runtime still require verification on an NVIDIA-equipped host; the recorded local environment covers generation and validation only.

## Jobs, ranks, threads, and GPUs

These are four different controls. [Local task options](/docs/task-options/) keep them separate:

```json
{
  "jobs": 8,
  "ranks": 4,
  "threads": 2,
  "gpus": 1,
  "launcher": ["mpiexec", "-n", "{ranks}"],
  "args": [],
  "env": {}
}
```

`jobs` controls build parallelism; `threads` sets `OMP_NUM_THREADS`; `ranks` is substituted into an explicit launcher. `gpus` is a hook parameter for a scheduler or launcher, not automatic GPU allocation. The MPI CTest uses two ranks; OpenMP's CTest sets two threads independently of your interactive Run settings.

## Tests, profiling, and clusters

Profile correctness tests carry `runtime` and profile labels. The CUDA executable returns 77 when no device is available, which CTest reports as skipped. CTest itself may exit 0 even with that skip. Inspect the named runtime test; successful compilation or a passing core test does not establish GPU correctness.

CPU and OpenMP tests were executed locally on macOS. CUDA and MPI generation/validation are covered, but their runtime checks still need NVIDIA/MPI hosts. Keep those jobs in your own CI or cluster qualification process.

Benchmark, profiler, and Slurm commands are explicit argv hooks. They run only when you select that task; Rivet does not submit jobs, poll a cluster, or cancel jobs in the background. See [Task options](/docs/task-options/) for examples and job IDs.
